The infrared laser power used in previous hybrid welding experiments was 1kW to 5kW, which is still lower than the power used for copper welding based on pure infrared lasers. The blue light power required in the hybrid welding process usually does not exceed 1kW, and sometimes even only 500W (welding thinner workpieces), although the use of 500W pure blue welding is not enough to achieve deep penetration welding.
If the composite beam cannot be successful in welding, then these efficiency advantages are of little use. However, all tests based on this new method have proved that hybrid welding has achieved unprecedented results in terms of welding depth, process stability and weld quality.
Figure 8: The formed weld is very smooth, with no defects on the edge of the weld
However, at present, the infrared laser still cannot avoid the spatter problem and the occasional collapse of the steam capillary in the deep melt welding of copper, and the processing technology is still not stable enough. In order to meet this challenge, further technological research is being carried out from factors such as spot size, shielding gas, and feed rate.
There is no doubt that Laserline’s hybrid welding solution not only provides new possibilities for welding traditional plates and thicker materials, but is also very suitable for welding stator coils in motors. For example, when using a 5kW infrared laser to weld the lugs, the molten pool is unstable, and a lot of splashes will be generated, and the workpiece cannot be used after cooling (Figure 9).
