As a cloud architect and gamer, I naturally have a considerable interest in cloud gaming. How does it work?
Does it work better or worse than regular gaming?
The term cloud gaming hides a simple principle: The computing power required for a game, i.e., processor, RAM, hard disks i/o, and graphics card, is provided by the cloud. The user’s computer then only displays the result of this calculation in the cloud, similar to a web page loaded and displayed in the browser.
With public cloud gaming, you either pay for the game once or monthly or finance this model with in-game purchases such as a battle pass. Client Cloud Gaming
Cloud gaming for the client has not been around that long. Only with stronger internet bandwidths in average households can the vast and continuously flowing data streams of a game be transmitted.
The game data, especially the graphics, no longer comes from your hardware but from the actual client’s internet, which runs the game for you. You react to the transmitted images with your mouse, keyboard, or controller, and this data is sent back to the cloud, where it affects the game.
Let’s take a deeper dive to see how this works. In the following, we will always use the term “cloud gaming” to refer to “client cloud gaming.”
The following diagram shows all the components of client cloud gaming. Let’s start with you.
But, of course, there are specific minimum requirements, which we discuss below. In general, though, the main thing is that you can display the streamed image, have internet, and interact with the game somehow, for example, with a mouse and keyboard.
But if we are talking about multiplayer shooters or open-world games, where many graphics have to be calculated, then a game server would collapse directly under many players. In principle, the cloud gaming provider creates a separate client for you in the cloud.
Because a lot of graphics have to be calculated, these instances are also assigned extra GPUs.
So this is where the biggest magic that makes cloud gaming possible happens.
The core is the game server, where the game, i.e., the application, runs. The server receives the requests and data from the clients or player instances, processes them, and sends the result back.
The only advantage here is, again, horizontal scaling in the cloud. For example, suppose suddenly many more players want to connect to the game, which may happen at certain times of the day.
The result will hopefully look like this:
What else do we need?
Some providers let you play for free at first. “NVIDIA Now” then limits you to a certain amount of playing time per day.
Cloud gaming is relatively inexpensive. Access to hundreds of games per month for a fixed flat rate is no higher than Netflix & Co.
Overall, as a cloud architect, I can state: Cloud gaming is safe.
Whether cloud gaming is worthwhile for you depends on your current hardware, which games you like to play, and whether your internet connection allows cloud gaming at all.
With almost all cloud gaming providers, you can access a massive collection of games. So even if you try multiple new games throughout the week, you won’t use one megabyte more than usual on your computer’s hard drive.
Monthly Games Flat Rate What has been standard for a few years now as a substitute for movies and TV, namely having access to hundreds of films and series for a monthly flat rate that you can stream for days at a stretch without any problems, is now arriving in the gaming world.
Graphics card, RAM, hard drive, etc. With cloud gaming, only one thing can happen to you: You have to buy a new mouse or controller if you have thrown the poor devices against the wall during a Rage-Quit
So you kill two birds with one stone. On the one hand, a hardware failure can no longer ruin your evening, and on the other hand, your game data, i.e., game saves and game configurations, are safely stored in the cloud.
You can play on your PC at your desk at home, comfortably play on your tablet on the couch in the evening, and stream a quick round of Tetris on your smartphone in bed. Everywhere you have the same game state with your account.
Cloud gaming also means that you cannot manipulate the client.
Play a game that is exclusively playable via cloud gaming and there shouldn’t be any cheaters anymore, in theory.
However, direct game manipulation (wallhack, aimbot, radar hacks, etc.) is out of the question because you always get the already calculated image streamed.
But, of course, many gamers like to tinker with their computers, and they are free to do so. Still, the casual gamer and the hardcore gamer who wants to play will be happy about cloud gaming.
There are no real drawbacks in all other genres.
Suppose you’re thinking about buying a new monitor for casual cloud gaming, and you’re flirting with a curved monitor. In that case, you’ll probably be interested in our article “Curved Gaming Monitors | Good for Gaming, Worth it, Cons & More”.
Nvidia is the leading developer of graphics cards for gaming and even if the competition AMD has a counterpart in all other technologies, NVIDIA Reflex is currently unique.
Already with the first 3D games with polygon graphics on PC, the question of the best graphics card came up immediately. Since I screwed most of my systems together myself, I can speak from experience.
By integrating directly into the game in question, Reflex Low Latency mode causes rendering to occur just-in-time, eliminating the GPU rendering queue and reducing CPU backlog. The Reflex technology leads to a reduction in latency.
The NVIDIA Reflex Latency Analyzer is a tool for measuring the system latency, i.e., the delay from the mouse click to the conversion to the monitor. Until now, such a measurement was only possible with costly equipment and is thus a truly innovative development from NVIDIA.
The best way to find out how this all works technically is to read about it directly on NVIDIAs Website here.
Low Latency Mode, however, can only act via the NVIDIA driver, while NVIDIA Reflex has been integrated directly into the games and thus works more smoothly. When using the Low Latency Mode, stuttering can occur, whereas NVIDIA Reflex, in my experience, doesn’t due to the better integration, and you can therefore activate NVIDIA Reflex without hesitation.
So far, only a few games can use NVIDIA Reflex, but new ones are constantly being added.
The On+Boost option causes the graphics card not to switch to energy-saving mode, and thus the graphics card clock remains high even under heavy CPU load. So far, however, tests have not shown that this leads to better performance than the On option in the game.
If you play one of the following FPS games, you might be interested in the appropriate article about NVIDIA Reflex for your game:
NVIDIA’s idea to work directly with game developers to reduce latency is, of course, excellent and the right approach.
However, many gamers were already celebrating NVIDIA Reflex as a revolution in Esports. In my experience, the effect is far from being that great, and no previous tests prove this.
If you have problems with stuttering and frames per second (FPS), there are many possible causes. In these posts we will show you how important it is to fix such a problem:
I’ve had a gaming laptop from a well-known manufacturer for two years now, with good performance to begin with. Before you buy a gaming laptop, you probably want to know if a gaming laptop really has enough power and other features to make gaming an absolute pleasure.
In addition, a growing number of games now support DLSS as well. I have used many technical tips and tricks and tested many features from hardware manufacturers in over 20 years of competitive gaming, including Apex Legends.
DLSS is supposed to have precisely this effect, according to NVIDIA, and that’s why I immediately tested it with different games. To the question of whether to enable DLSS in Apex, I’ll give you a condensed answer first:
Different games were compared with and without DLSS enabled in this Youtube video. Naturally, your hardware configuration is guaranteed to be different and thus produce different results, but for a first impression, the video is interesting:
Many comparative tests of various FPS games show that DLSS really positively influences the input latency.
No one can tell you how positively DLSS will affect your configuration and thus the game you play.
In general, DLSS 2.X increases the number of frames per second (FPS) of a supported video game. Tests with many other FPS games show that it depends on many hardware factors how significant the influence of DLSS on the FPS is.
Numerous tests (and I don’t mean the marketing material from NVIDIA) have proven that DLSS enables more FPS in every supported game.
The result is incredibly individual, so I can only recommend simply enabling DLSS and measuring the FPS baseline beforehand.
As mentioned in the previous point, DLSS in performance mode is a trade-off. It reduces graphics quality and thus reduces latency and gains FPS.
So the graphics quality is actually reduced, but in the best case, it’s hidden so that you as a player don’t notice any difference.
Enable DLSS in Performance mode, and you’ll instantly see if the graphics quality changes for your eyes.
Comparisons between FSR and DLSS bring clear results based only on the hardware used and the game played. For you personally, you can’t derive anything concrete from the results unless you happen to have the same conditions 1:1.
For any supported game, I can only recommend enabling DLSS. You either want to gain more performance in the form of more FPS as a competitive gamer or gain higher resolution with the same number of FPS as a casual gamer.
