Pro gamers prefer crumb-free, dry, and balanced finger foods that don’t negatively impact gaming performance. Casual gamers and streamers mainly eat quickly prepared food (microwave) or frozen convenience food that is highly satiating.
With one winking and one serious eye, let’s dive into the exciting topic of “nutrition for gamers”, ok?
If pizza and Coke come to mind when you think of gamers, then I have to bring you down to earth a bit. The clichés have long since been disproved, and current studies also show that a large proportion of gamers eat a relatively healthy diet.
Among them were 35 professional gamers or former professional gamers, 190 semi-professional gamers, and 592 casual gamers. The average age of the participants in the study was 24 years, which is more or less the same age as the performance peak of an esport gamer (see our article Age of Pro Gamers | Youngest | Average | Oldest (2021)).
Limit values for the consumption of alcoholic beverages are not exceeded
Less sugar is consumed in food, but more sugar is consumed in energy drinks. On average, however, no more than the rest of the population.
Almost half of the participants cook for themselves at least 5 days a week.
The Body Mass Index is within the normal range for all participant groups, from professionals to casual gamers, at over 50% in each case. However, in the case of the professionals, it was even significantly higher at 70%, which is undoubtedly due to the demands placed on competitive athletes today.
In summary, the study states that gamers are pretty sporty and eat a healthy diet. Of course, there is room for improvement, and the topic of exercise should be given an even higher priority to compensate for the many hours of sitting.
Now that we’ve shed some light on the current state of science let’s look at the real world. What do gamers prefer to eat and drink, and why?
Pro gamers are primarily oriented toward the eating habits of competitive athletes. But, of course, the standard of what constitutes “good” food differs in some respects from the views of a general gamer.
With finger food, the food should not be dripping with grease – greasy fingers on the mouse or keyboard are a no-go. Examples: Chicken wings or ribs
Food must not crumble – Again, keyboards don’t like crumbs. Neither do mouse sensors, for that matter.
If we look again at the two groupings, the pro gamer naturally focuses on food supporting performance.
Performance has to be on point, high communication, and above all, consistency has to be shown.
Counter-examples would be ripe fruit or very juicy fruit. For example, plums, ripe pears, peaches, or similar often drip when bitten off.
What do you consume as a gamer per day?
The most famous example is strawberry yogurt, which does not contain strawberries, but wood chips that taste like strawberries.
It’s similar to energy drinks. As we have shown in the article, Do Energy Drinks Boost Gaming Skills?
Your blood sugar is raised to a good value in the first moment, and your hunger is gone. After a short time, the body realizes that it has not received any substantial food and lowers the blood sugar again.
We have shown you a few examples of do’s and don’t in this article.
The psyllium husks and the oat bran will swell, so wait 5 minutes. Then, finally, add another shot of milk.
