Over the past few years, Masakari and I have switched entirely to wireless equipment for mice and keyboards. However, when choosing a new mouse or keyboard, we kept coming across Logitech Unifying technology.
A wireless technology that allows multiple mice and keyboards to be connected to a PC. The devices are connected and managed using the Logitech Unifying Software.
Here you can download the software from Logitech’s website.
Even though the receiver has a similar range and the same frequency range of 2.4 GHz as Bluetooth, Logitech Unifying is a separate and therefore incompatible technology.
The technology is comparable to Bluetooth. However, through Logitech’s proprietary Unify protocol, only Logitech devices that support Unifying technology can pair with a receiver.
Neither technology is better than the other. Unifying technology is also based on the 2.4 GHz frequency range, and the technical specifications hardly differ from Bluetooth.
How Many Devices Does Logitech Unifying Receiver Support?
The Logitech receiver does not use Bluetooth technology. As a result, only devices that support Logitech’s Unifying technology can be paired.
You can pair a Logitech mouse with Unifying technology with any Logitech Unifying USB Receiver. Pairing requires the Logitech Unifying software.
Logitech Unifying USB Receivers only work with devices that support Unifying technology. You cannot pair other devices with a Logitech dongle.
The Logitech Unifying Receiver can be purchased for $15-$25 and paired with existing devices with Unifying technology.
