Would you like to know how to make patterns or prints to apply them to countless products?
A pattern or print consists of a set of basic repeating units called rapports. Thus, by being assembled in this way, we ensure that there is a very wide continuity of a pattern to be applied on any surface, without the loss of quality that would occur if an illustration were greatly enlarged.
Once we have created this basic unit (which we will have saved as Inteligent object to be able to modify it later), we can create a new document and copy our repeated rapport in any way we want, for example by forming a grid. But here comes the problem: we have gaps between the rapports and the grid shape is noticeable.
It is important that our pattern has a continuity, that is, that the gaps are not seen so clearly (unless we want this specific design). How can we solve it?
To solve the mentioned problem, we have two ways:
We place the drawing that we want to be in the gap between units outside the canvas. This drawing will be, for example, in the 1 layer.
To do this we will select it and drag it to the lower icon of Duplicate layer, creating the Layer 1 copy.
With the layer grouped (we have combined the previously visible layers), we adjust the size of the image to put one that is easier for us to handle. For example 5000 x 5000 px.
We combine the visible layers of the rapport that we have created and converted it to Inteligent object.
And we have already assembled our pattern. In this case, we have made a pattern in the form of a grid, which is the simplest, but there are a multitude of forms.
In this pattern the drawings have no foot, that is, if we rotate it it works just as well. The use of it will allow us that, for example in textile design, the seams look good, whatever we put the pattern.
What are you waiting for to let your imagination run wild creating beautiful patterns?
