And it is not a way of understanding the placement of a series of elements in a photograph that was born years ago, but rather centuries ago. It is Leonardo Pisano, also known as Fibonacci, who disclosed this rule, which at the time this Italian mathematician called the Fibonnaci Succession, and which today we know as the golden ratio.
1 What is the golden ratio?
2.1 In Greek architecture
6 Web tools for the Golden Ratio
As many say, mathematics is everything and it is worth wondering what use are these accounts for what would be their application in art. We would have to face a rectangle in which its sides measure two of the numbers of the Fibonacci series.
It is that spiral that is called the Golden Spiral or Golden Spiral, and that, in a very curious way, we can find it in a multitude of ways in nature. We just have to take a look around, and we will see that the seeds of a sunflower follow the lines of the Golden Spiral to our greater amazement.
The "Golden ratio", as it is called in English, is approximately at a ratio equal to 1: 1.61 and we can represent it with the "golden rectangle", which consists of a square and a smaller rectangle. If we remove the square from the rectangle, we will be left with another "golden rectangle."
We have to go back to at least 4.000 years ago when the Golden Ratio began to be used. And it is that even some historians go back to the ancient Egyptians, who already used this principle to build the pyramids.
The great Leonardo da Vinci, like other artists of many eras, used the "golden ratio" in this well-known work. The figures that characterize the composition are taken to less than two-thirds, and the position of Jesus is perfectly located to fit the Golden ratio of the entire canvas.
La nature makes use of this golden ratio as a symbol of its own of how universal this methodology has. We can see it everywhere, as the video shows, such as in flowers, dragonflies or sea shells.
The first thing is to draw a square.
Se draw a diagonal from one of the center corners, the one below, to the other in the opposite corner.
We create a rectangle from the new horizontal line and follow the horizontal rectangle.
Curiosities about the golden ratio The Fibonacci sequence, arithmetic relative of the golden number, has a rather striking curiosity and is related to the reproduction of rabbits raised by Leonardo Pisano Bionacci himself in his Book of the Abacus in 1202.
Another curiosity, and related to the human body, is the ability to determine if a patient's uterus looks normal when using the golden ratio. Its height is divided by its width, so that the result is close to 1,618.
Another big one Antonio Stradivarius used the golden ratio to place the openings on his violins to be on par with that number. What is unknown is whether the situation of the openings had to do with the quality of the sound, although it does seem that it was a more aesthetic thing.
