History of graphic design: evolution and most important events | Creatives Online
Its history does not have an established date but is determined by a series of events that have made its evolution possible. To put you in situation, we are going to transfer you to nineteenth century, a century full of events that began the first movements and therefore were the first pillars where the design began to be built as we see it today.
They use elements of nature to enhance their works
They explore asymmetry and represent it in their works
The artists that are part of this school are:
He is considered one of the most influential artists of the XNUMXth century, stands out for working on the phenomena of abstraction and among his works stands out: Cat and Bird and Castle and Sun.
He was an expressionist, abstract painter and a great master of color and design. Composition Eight and the Blue Rider stand out.
Artist and dominant of dynamic compositions where he worked with horizontal and vertical lines. Among his works are: Articulated Wall and Double Ascension.
Source: The Eye in the Sky
In the previous post, we mentioned a man named Gutemberg But everything does not stay there. Typography has also had its evolution and precisely, it began to have it in the century XX, specifically in the 30s.
And what did the designers do this time?
In times of war, especially in the First World War, the war poster, also called political or propaganda, was created. This poster was created with the aim of convince and at the same time manipulate society to get them to unify the government.
The designers of these posters resorted to what we call rhetoric and used a connotative communicational tone, thus appealing to the emotions and feelings
They put the text before the image, that is, the text took on much more prominence due to its peculiar headlines
The posters belonging to the Second World War, aesthetically continued the same graphic line and characteristics. Among the poster artists who had the greatest influence at the time, the following stand out:
Alexander was the most representative poster artist, he was characterized by using geometric figures that were accompanied by light colors. Many of his works create movements in his composition due to the way in which he superimposed the elements (text - image).
In Europe, especially in Switzerland, several movements were created but the best known was undoubtedly in Swiss international style. This style originated in the 50s and lasted until the 70s.
One of the main characteristics is that they managed to unify the asymmetry in the compositions, this was achieved with the use of typographic grids and grids.
Over time, Swiss schools were formed in cities such as Geneva, Lausanne or Zurich.
So far, we have concluded that Europe was one of the most important cradles in the world of art and design. But the story does not end here, America was considered a great world power in interwar and political times.
This school was a summary of the artistic influence that the Americans represented in the design. A much more representative design, with graphic lines released and far from the static and the moral.
We find features such as:
And where did this movement come from? After many wars and social, economic and political conflicts, many artists felt the need to create a style that would express the interior of the human being.
Striking colors that come from social movements (hippies)
The 60s and 70s in Europe were conditioned by the famous Cold War. The cold war triggered confrontations between countries like the United States and the USSR.
For this reason, the English style came from the hand of the posters designed by Jamie reid. His posters are moved by the punk movement, creating in this way graphic elements full of striking and energetic colors and he was a participant in the band The Sex Pistols to design many of his album covers.
In 1966 he created one of the most representative works for graphic design of the time: a poster that derived from one of the singer's albums Bob Dylan. Years later, he designed The New York magazine together with one of his most famous projects: I Love New York.
He was one of the quintessential typographic artists of the 80s. His designs are influenced by the Swiss style and he has been a source of inspiration for many artists.
You should know that all the evolution continues in our designs every day. When designing a corporate identity, a poster, a cover for a magazine or even when we photograph and draw, We need to be inspired by those artists who have already written their own history and left their mark.
Conclusion Everyone at some point in our life when we design, we also create a concept from scratch and that concept is our origin and our evolution.
