In this article we are going to tell you everything you need to know about the Chondrichthyes, their characteristics and biology.
Body: A spindle-shaped body with a pointed face with an abdomen in front. The tail of the body has an abnormal closed tail, that is, there are two different shapes and structures of leaves, one of which contains the end of the spine, and the front one has a pair of pectoral fins, a pair of pelvic fins, and two dorsal.
Teeth: The teeth do not merge with the lower jaw, there are two rows, the last row replaces the missing teeth in the first row, so new teeth can always grow. Depending on the species, these can have a serrated shape to cut food, sharp and have a gripping function, in the case of striped species, they have flat teeth that can be scratched on the surface.
Body : They have a very strange shape, their body is elongated and their heads protruding, they have a classic structure that can support the females during mating. Its nose is like a rabbit and its tail is like a whip.
Size: They can be up to 2 meters long.
Elasmobranchs have dermal scales, which allow them to reduce turbulence when swimming. On the other hand, along with their lipid-rich liver, their ability to swallow air, and their fins, they are excellent swimmers and these adaptations allow them to stay in the water.
Their tails are whip-shaped, with one or more spines at the end, which are connected to the poisonous glands of certain species. They also have electrical organs on both sides of their heads, which can produce electrical shocks and stun their prey or predators.
Viviparous: They will develop a real placenta from which the embryo can eat. This mode of reproduction promoted their evolutionary success in this group.
