3 The most polluted country in the world
There are other forms of air pollution that are not easily detectable or visible, but also have deadly consequences for the health of living things and ecosystems. These pollutants are causing warming and catastrophic consequences for the planet.
Among these emissions we find gases emitted during volcanic eruptions, but their effects were not permanent. However, with the advent of the Human Industrial Revolution and intensifying population growth, we are faced with a global landscape of air pollution.
It is estimated that about 3% of hospitalizations are caused by exacerbations of related diseases with the amount of pollutants in the atmosphere. The most polluted countries in the world are those with the highest concentrations of these gases and therefore the greatest impacts on health.
If we don't know, we should know that current levels of pollution haven't occurred in at least 3 million years without humans on Earth. It should also be remembered that at that time the Earth was going through a period of very active volcanism.
China, with more than 10.065 million tons of carbon dioxide emissions
An average of 97,10 polluting particles has been reached. This amount is due in part to the more than 166 million people living in Bangladesh are responsible for the massive emissions.
It not only increases the amount of polluting gas, but also decreases the absorption of carbon dioxide by plants.
4 Main differences
It is estimated that more than 70% of the country's soil is physically, chemically or biologically degraded Due to intensive agriculture, the inadequate use of agricultural inputs, the removal of crop residues and the absence of organic fertilizers, its soil deteriorates day by day. The main problems in the degradation of this natural resource are related to erosion, salinization and reduction of organic stocks, as well as compaction caused by overcropping of agricultural machinery.
After land use changes in terrestrial ecosystems, the number of plant species is drastically reduced and the quantity and quality of their resources varies with different root systems. This results in lower soil organic matter content, which in turn limits soil biodiversity and food availability.
Based on the above, there is a need to develop technologies that can increase soil organic reserves, conserve soil moisture, improve fertilizer use efficiency, and reduce soil and water pollution. Numerous studies have been conducted on the use of biofertilizers as an option to improve soil conditions and increase yields.
These biofertilizers are gaining ground over common fertilizers since the environmental impacts are greatly reduced. Thanks to the use of microorganisms capable of improving soil conditions and its quality, it is possible to obtain improvements in crop yields without the need to damage the soil.
On the other hand, chemical fertilizers contribute to desertification and can cause almost permanent loss of soil. In addition, the use of biofertilizers helps in the regeneration of soils and crops, where organic matter and microorganisms can fix nutrients and improve soil structure.
